    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d316">

                <pb facs="0254" n="250" sameAs="frus:frus1938v04/pg250"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">393.115/169</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">President Roosevelt
                        to the Secretary of State</hi><anchor n="34"
                        xml:id="d316-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-01-28">January 28, 1938</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>The January 26th dispatch from Gauss, No. 142,<anchor n="35" xml:id="d316-f2"/>
                    and similar dispatches relating to looting of American-owned property in China
                    is bringing to a head the question of indemnities for lost property.</p>
                <p>Under no circumstances can the major part of the looting be called a necessary
                    result of armed conflict. The buildings were not destroyed by usual fire, by
                    bombs, nor were the buildings or property to which I refer destroyed or damaged
                    as a necessary part of military operations either offensive or defensive.</p>
                <p>If you have not already begun to do so, I think we should start to lay the
                    foundation for holding Japan accountable in dollars for the acts of her
                    soldiers. Perhaps we should not suggest it until after our case has been firmly
                    established but it is a fact that there is a vast amount of Japanese owned
                    property in the United States and that we have excellent precedent in the Alien
                    Property Custodian Act<anchor n="36" xml:id="d316-f3"/> for holding this
                    property in escrow. Enough said!</p>
                <closer><signed>F[<hi rendition="#small-caps">ranklin</hi>] D. R[<hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">oosevelt</hi>]</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d316-f1" n="34" type="source" xml:id="d316-f1-content">
                <p>Notation on the original by the Secretary instructed the Legal Adviser,
                    Hack-worth, to consult the Division of Far Eastern Affairs “and act.”</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d316-f2" n="35" xml:id="d316-f2-content">
                <p><hi rendition="#italic">Supra.</hi></p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d316-f3" n="36" xml:id="d316-f3-content">
                <p>“Trading with the Enemy Act,” approved October 6, 1917; 40 Stat. 411.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
